markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Facilitating the History Discussion

This lesson moves from partner discussion into independent research, then into forward-looking prediction. Watch for two different kinds of stuck thinking along the way, and use discipline-based reasoning as your throughline for both.

## What's Actually Happening in This Lesson

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 16px;">
    <div style="background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; font-size: 12px; width: 24px; height: 24px; min-width: 24px; border-radius: 6px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;">1</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>Warm-up discussion:</strong> partners pick one AI technology (voice assistants, video recommendations, AI-generated art, self-driving cars) and discuss what makes it "intelligent" and what inventions led to it</div>
  </div>
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 16px;">
    <div style="background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; font-size: 12px; width: 24px; height: 24px; min-width: 24px; border-radius: 6px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;">2</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>Interdisciplinary framing:</strong> you introduce AI as the product of research across computer science, psychology, neurology, and linguistics</div>
  </div>
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 16px;">
    <div style="background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; font-size: 12px; width: 24px; height: 24px; min-width: 24px; border-radius: 6px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;">3</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>Explore AI Milestones:</strong> students choose one discipline and research a related AI innovation using AI Chat</div>
  </div>
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px;">
    <div style="background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; font-size: 12px; width: 24px; height: 24px; min-width: 24px; border-radius: 6px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;">4</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>Predict the Future:</strong> in pairs, students read existing predictions, discuss what feels realistic, then write their own</div>
  </div>
</div>

<hr>

## The Same Pattern as Lesson 1, in a New Context

Just like the Intelligence Card Sort, students default to describing what a technology *does*, not how it *works*. Same stuck-thinking pattern, new material.

<div style="display: flex; gap: 16px; margin: 20px 0; flex-wrap: wrap;">
  <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 250px; background-color: #FFE3CE; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <div style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; color: #611D00; margin-bottom: 10px;">Stuck: Output-Only</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #611D00; line-height: 1.6;">
      "Voice assistants answer questions."
    </div>
  </div>
  <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 250px; background-color: #CCF1D0; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <div style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; color: #003F25; margin-bottom: 10px;">Strong: Names the Process</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #003F25; line-height: 1.6;">
      "It has to figure out what words you said, then figure out what you're actually asking."
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#128161; Language to borrow:</strong>
  </div>
  <div style="font-size: 13px; font-weight: bold; color: #4C42CF; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px; margin-top: 16px; margin-bottom: 8px;">For an output-only description</div>
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; margin-bottom: 8px;">&bull; "How does the voice assistant know what words you said? How does it understand what your question means?"</div>
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; margin-bottom: 8px;">&bull; "What does the computer have to figure out, step by step?"</div>
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222;">&bull; "What had to exist before someone could build this?"</div>
</div>

<hr>

## Watch for: Vague Predictions

When students write their own prediction for the future of AI, expect answers that sound thoughtful but say nothing concrete, "scientists need to figure it out," or "we need better computers."

<div style="background-color: #FFE3CE; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #611D00; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#9888; Why this happens:</strong> students haven't yet connected "prediction" back to the same discipline-based reasoning they just used in Explore AI Milestones. The fix isn't a better prompt, it's reminding them of a method they already used.
  </div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#128161; Language to borrow:</strong>
  </div>
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 8px;">&bull; "Each step in AI's history came from someone solving a particular problem in a particular discipline. What discipline could contribute to this innovation?"</div>
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222;">&bull; "What questions might people in that discipline need to ask?"</div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin-top: 16px;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;">
    One nuance worth knowing: students don't need to predict within the same discipline they chose for Explore AI Milestones. A student who researched Neurology is free to land on a Linguistics-flavored prediction. It's the reasoning method that carries over, not the specific field.
  </div>
</div>
MARKDOWN
